        <body>
            <div xml:id="d33">

                <pb facs="0173" n="49" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg49"/>

                <head>No. 33.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Kasson to Mr. Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-03-30"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 30, 1881</date>.
                        (Received April 20.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 446.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In order to complete the information which
                    I have believed to be interesting to the Department of State at Washington, I
                    have procured copies of the official regulations established by the ministry of
                    foreign affairs in Vienna for the examination and admission of candidates for
                    appointment into the diplomatic and consular service.</p>
                <p>The original and translations are transmitted herewith.</p>
                <p>The rigidity and thoroughness of the examinations will compare well with those of
                    undergraduates at our best institutions of learning.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN A. KASSON.</signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d33-a1.xml" xpointer="d33-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d33-a2.xml" xpointer="d33-a2"/>
